WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:05.931
A project delivers an output: a readiness check, a rebuilt line, a planning tool.

2
00:00:06.948 --> 00:00:09.618
A programme runs projects together that deliver

3
00:00:09.688 --> 00:00:11.952
one outcome none of them reaches alone:

4
00:00:12.079 --> 00:00:14.843
cancelled jobs from 14 to 8 percent.

5
00:00:15.877 --> 00:00:18.640
If you can drop one and the goal still holds,

6
00:00:18.814 --> 00:00:20.509
it belongs in the portfolio:

7
00:00:20.613 --> 00:00:23.841
all the projects competing for the same money and people.

8
00:00:25.176 --> 00:00:29.089
A dependency: B needs A's data definition by week 10,

9
00:00:29.309 --> 00:00:32.653
and C wants B's two planners in the second quarter.

10
00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.546
Keep one list: owner, date, and what happens if it slips.

11
00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:42.275
Four independent projects, each 90 percent on time,

12
00:00:42.414 --> 00:00:45.259
all make it only 66 percent of the time.

13
00:00:46.265 --> 00:00:49.318
The check reads data team B is redesigning.

14
00:00:50.039 --> 00:00:55.671
On one page: B freezes the definition by week 6, IT tests before go-live.

15
00:00:57.010 --> 00:01:01.480
The programme board: the sponsor, the project owners, the outcome's owner,

16
00:01:01.689 --> 00:01:04.592
and finance if money moves between projects.

17
00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:10.311
Monthly, the board decides on the seams: sequencing, shared people, scope.

18
00:01:11.031 --> 00:01:14.491
Decisions inside a project stay in its steering group.

19
00:01:15.569 --> 00:01:18.889
Watermelon reporting: green outside, red inside.

20
00:01:19.639 --> 00:01:23.830
All three report green, cancellations are still at 14 percent.

21
00:01:24.897 --> 00:01:29.182
Report the outcome number itself monthly, with each project's share.

22
00:01:30.186 --> 00:01:33.842
You own the story, the list and the rhythm, not the projects.

23
00:01:34.542 --> 00:01:38.942
Ask: which number moves, how much, and which project moves which part?

24
00:01:40.286 --> 00:01:44.896
Score roughly, 1 to 5, on value, effort, risk and strategy.

25
00:01:45.561 --> 00:01:47.929
Whoever sets the weights sets the outcome.

26
00:01:48.986 --> 00:01:54.198
The bottleneck limits how much gets done: usually a few scarce experts, not money.

27
00:01:55.185 --> 00:01:57.275
So count in value per scarce day.

28
00:01:57.963 --> 00:02:02.107
A brings two and a half thousand euros a day, B five thousand.

29
00:02:02.845 --> 00:02:05.237
B goes first, although A looks bigger.

30
00:02:06.247 --> 00:02:07.652
The risk-adjusted value:

31
00:02:07.756 --> 00:02:13.213
300 thousand at 50 percent confidence counts as 150 thousand.

32
00:02:14.546 --> 00:02:20.746
Count the scarce people: two engineers with 120 days each give 240.

33
00:02:21.761 --> 00:02:23.886
A food producer wants all five.

34
00:02:24.603 --> 00:02:29.374
Per engineer day: B four and a half thousand, D three, A two and a half,

35
00:02:29.526 --> 00:02:34.320
and C, a new MES for the orders on the floor, one at 50 percent risk.

36
00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:40.611
Rank, and draw the capacity line where the running total reaches 240.

37
00:02:41.301 --> 00:02:42.764
Everything below waits.

38
00:02:43.774 --> 00:02:46.676
B, D and A: 150 days.

39
00:02:47.384 --> 00:02:51.088
C first gets a 60-day phase that tests the big assumption.

40
00:02:51.807 --> 00:02:54.431
210 in all, 30 as buffer.

41
00:02:55.458 --> 00:03:00.288
For E, ask the director for a number: above two and a half thousand a day,

42
00:03:00.485 --> 00:03:01.449
it goes ahead of A.

43
00:03:02.164 --> 00:03:03.255
The team chooses.

44
00:03:03.976 --> 00:03:06.263
Trap: by March the line is ignored.

45
00:03:07.618 --> 00:03:11.264
Work in progress is everything started and not yet finished.

46
00:03:12.359 --> 00:03:18.431
Little's law, 1961: lead time is work in progress divided by throughput.

47
00:03:19.349 --> 00:03:24.504
At three finished a quarter, twelve open projects take four quarters, six take two.

48
00:03:25.546 --> 00:03:27.660
Switching and waiting cut throughput too:

49
00:03:27.764 --> 00:03:31.944
twelve projects at half speed deliver less than six at full speed.

50
00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:37.783
Never a bare no, but: then project B stops, or slips three months.

51
00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:39.489
Which do you prefer?

52
00:03:40.481 --> 00:03:42.223
Whoever owns both chooses.

53
00:03:42.934 --> 00:03:44.664
Keep a visible waiting list.

54
00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:48.041
Ask: what do we stop to make room for this?

55
00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:51.591
Usually it is the same five people.

56
00:03:52.300 --> 00:03:56.828
A capacity matrix puts names down, projects across, days per month,

57
00:03:57.014 --> 00:03:58.314
and their real days.

58
00:03:59.319 --> 00:04:03.545
Overbooked rows are resource conflicts: more days promised than there are.

59
00:04:04.246 --> 00:04:07.358
Two or three red rows explain most of the delay.

60
00:04:08.398 --> 00:04:14.029
Real availability is lower than you think: of 260 weekdays, 50 go to leave,

61
00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:17.093
sickness and training, and 20 to meetings.

62
00:04:17.837 --> 00:04:19.544
190 remain.

63
00:04:20.581 --> 00:04:25.493
With 70 percent day job, 57 project days are left, five a month.

64
00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.784
A context switch costs time.

65
00:04:29.485 --> 00:04:37.763
Gerald Weinberg estimated in 1992: two projects 20 percent lost, three 40, five 75.

66
00:04:38.466 --> 00:04:40.475
An estimate, but recognisable.

67
00:04:41.492 --> 00:04:46.136
Better: fewer at once, in real blocks, such as Tuesday and Thursday for A.

68
00:04:47.154 --> 00:04:51.090
The owner chooses: Jan does A and B and C starts in May,

69
00:04:51.253 --> 00:04:53.854
or a contractor does C for 40 thousand.

70
00:04:54.898 --> 00:04:58.404
Don't solve it quietly with evenings: that hides the cost.

71
00:04:59.436 --> 00:05:02.652
Some book to 80 percent, others plan a quarter ahead.

72
00:05:03.393 --> 00:05:06.528
Whose days are we counting on, and has she seen this plan?

73
00:05:07.834 --> 00:05:10.934
Planner Fatima is on four projects, yours too.

74
00:05:11.624 --> 00:05:15.420
Each assumes a day a week: 16 to 17 days a month.

75
00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:16.903
She has 4.

76
00:05:17.915 --> 00:05:22.989
Your pilot needs her for 8 days in March, and two other leads count on March too.

77
00:05:23.995 --> 00:05:27.396
Build the matrix, ask Fatima first what is realistic,

78
00:05:27.594 --> 00:05:30.346
and go to whoever owns all four, not to her.

79
00:05:31.370 --> 00:05:36.722
Options: the rest to April and May, a planner from the regional office for 10 days,

80
00:05:36.943 --> 00:05:38.197
or the pilot later.

81
00:05:38.987 --> 00:05:41.901
Then everyone plans on the decision, not hope.
